title: Socrates_Join
---
Socrates: Captain! Where are you going, and where have you come from?
Captain: Nowhere. Away from here.
Socrates: Away from here. And yet you walk with great purpose, for a man going nowhere.
Captain: I wanted to think. Alone.
Socrates: Alone! Then I have arrived at exactly the wrong moment, which is my custom.
Captain: You don't seem sorry.
Socrates: I am never sorry to interrupt a man who is about to think. Thinking alone is how most bad thinking gets done.
Captain: And thinking with a stranger is better?
Socrates: Immeasurably. A stranger will ask you what you mean. You will not.
Captain: I'm not going to be rid of you, am I.
Socrates: Not before that plane tree, at any rate. Do you see it? The tall one, past the flowers.
Captain: I see it.
Socrates: There is shade there, and I am told the ground is soft. Lead on — I'll keep up.
===

title: Walk_1
---
Socrates: Tell me — what were you going to think about, before I ruined it?
Captain: I don't know yet. That's why I was going to think.
Socrates: A man walks out of the city to consider a question he does not yet have. Remarkable.
Captain: You're mocking me.
Socrates: I'm envying you. I have far too many questions. You have room for one good one.
===

title: Walk_2
---
Captain: You said thinking alone is how bad thinking gets done. Prove it.
Socrates: I can't prove it. I can only ask whether you have ever been wrong.
Captain: Of course I have.
Socrates: And while you were wrong — did it feel any different from being right?
Captain: ...No.
Socrates: That is the whole difficulty. And we are not even at the tree yet.
===

title: Walk_3
---
Socrates: Mind the roots here.
Captain: You're not even winded.
Socrates: I walk very little, but I argue constantly. It is the same exercise.
===

title: PlaneTree
---
Socrates: There. Sit, if you like. I'll stand — I think better on my feet and worse when I'm comfortable.
Socrates: Now. You came out here to think, and you hadn't chosen a question. Let me lend you one.
Socrates: You believe you know a great many things. Your own name. The color of the sky. What you ate this morning.
Socrates: Do you know them? Or do you only believe them?
-> Captain: There's a difference?
	<<jump PlaneTree_Difference>>
-> Captain: I know them. I can see the sky right now.
	<<jump PlaneTree_Senses>>
-> Captain: Knowledge is belief that happens to be true, and that you have some reason for.
	<<jump PlaneTree_JTB>>
===

title: PlaneTree_Difference
---
Socrates: An excellent question, and one I would much rather you asked than answered.
Socrates: Suppose you believe the hull will hold. And it holds. Were you right, or were you lucky?
Captain: ...Both, I suppose.
Socrates: Then tell me which of the two you would want your crew to have.
	<<jump PlaneTree_ToChalmers>>
===

title: PlaneTree_Senses
---
Socrates: You can see it. And have your eyes never lied to you?
Captain: Rarely.
Socrates: Rarely is an interesting word. It means yes.
Socrates: When you dream, does the dream announce itself as a dream?
Captain: No.
Socrates: Then at night your eyes lie to you perfectly, and you believe them completely. What is different now?
	<<jump PlaneTree_ToChalmers>>
===

title: PlaneTree_JTB
---
Socrates: Careful — you have answered too well. Men who answer well stop looking.
Socrates: Suppose you watch your navigator go below deck. You believe he is below. You have reason: you saw him go.
Socrates: But he climbed out the far hatch, and he is standing behind you at this moment.
Captain: Then I was wrong.
Socrates: And yet a man is below deck. Another of the same name, whom you have never met, is down there now.
Socrates: Your belief was true. You had your reason. Did you know?
	<<jump PlaneTree_ToChalmers>>
===

title: PlaneTree_ToChalmers
tags: 
position: null,null
---
Captain: You're not going to answer any of this, are you.
Socrates: I have never answered anything. It is the only discipline I have kept.
Socrates: But I can tell you where to go next.
Socrates: There is a man in these woods who has thought about this longer than I have been dead. He is called Chalmers.
Captain: You're dead?
Socrates: Let us call that his question rather than mine.
Socrates: Go and find him. Ask him what you asked me. He is far more generous with answers than I am — you may come to find that a mixed blessing.
Captain: You're not coming?
Socrates: I have walked far enough. And you meant to think alone. Go on — you are better prepared for it now.
===
